  <section class="appointment-definition-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">The direct answer</div>
      <h2>What are B2B appointment setting services?</h2>
      <p><strong>B2B appointment setting services identify relevant business accounts and decision-makers, start the outreach, handle follow-up, qualify whether a useful sales conversation exists, and schedule the agreed next step for your sales team.</strong></p>
      <p>The provider owns the work before the meeting: account selection, contact research, call preparation, outreach, live conversation, persistence, qualification, scheduling, and the transfer of useful information. Your sales team owns the opportunity after the handoff.</p>
      <div class="appointment-truth-line">A meeting is not qualified because a name appears on a calendar. It is qualified because both sides understand why the next conversation should happen.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="appointment-problem-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Why appointment volume fails</div>
      <h2>A full calendar can still produce an empty pipeline.</h2>
      <p class="appointment-section-intro">Appointment-setting campaigns break when the incentive is to book anything that moves. The calendar fills, sellers lose confidence, and the real problem stays hidden.</p>
      <div class="appointment-problem-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Wrong accounts</h3><p>The company was easy to reach but never fit the target market, business model, size, use case, or buying reality.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Wrong people</h3><p>The title looked close enough, but the person does not own the problem, influence the decision, or know who does.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>No business reason</h3><p>The prospect agreed to a vague introduction without a problem, priority, trigger, or outcome worth discussing.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>No buyer commitment</h3><p>The meeting was pushed onto the calendar before the prospect understood the relevance or agreed to a clear next step.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="qualified-meeting-standard" class="appointment-standard-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The CallTeam Qualified Meeting Standard</div>
      <h2>Agree on what counts before the first call is made.</h2>
      <p class="appointment-section-intro">Qualification is not one universal script. We define the standard around your offer, market, deal size, sales process, and what your sales team needs to know before accepting the meeting.</p>
      <div class="appointment-standard-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Account fit</h3><p>Industry, geography, company size, operating profile, technology, business model, or other attributes that make the company a plausible buyer.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Role relevance</h3><p>The contact owns, influences, uses, evaluates, or can credibly route the problem and the buying conversation.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Business reason</h3><p>A problem, goal, current approach, change, trigger, frustration, or commercial priority creates a legitimate reason to continue.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Interest and timing</h3><p>The prospect sees enough relevance to invest in the next conversation, even if the final buying timeline is not yet known.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Decision path</h3><p>Where useful, we capture the people, process, current solution, evaluation path, or constraints likely to shape the opportunity.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Clear next step</h3><p>The meeting has a defined purpose, the prospect knows what will be discussed, and your seller receives the context to prepare.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="appointment-standard-note"><strong>The rule:</strong> if the prospect is not ready for a meeting but deserves follow-up, we record the right next action instead of forcing the calendar.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

