  <section id="faq" class="appointment-faq-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">B2B appointment setting FAQ</div>
      <h2>Questions to ask before hiring an appointment-setting company.</h2>
      <div class="appointment-faq-list">
        <details><summary>What is B2B appointment setting?</summary><p>B2B appointment setting is the work of identifying relevant business accounts and decision-makers, starting a conversation, following up, qualifying whether a useful sales discussion exists, and scheduling the agreed next step for a sales team.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How is appointment setting different from lead generation?</summary><p>Lead generation is the broader process of identifying, attracting, contacting, and qualifying possible buyers. Appointment setting focuses on earning and scheduling the sales conversation. CallTeam can connect both: build or refine the prospect universe, conduct the outreach, qualify the response, and book the next step.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How does CallTeam qualify a sales appointment?</summary><p>CallTeam and the client agree on qualification criteria before launch. Depending on the offer, those criteria can include account fit, role relevance, a real business reason, interest or need, timing, current approach, decision path, and a clear next step.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does CallTeam provide the prospect list?</summary><p>Yes. CallTeam can build and refine a targeted B2B prospect list or work from an existing client list, CRM, event list, inbound lead source, target-account list, or dormant opportunity pool.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How are meetings confirmed and handed off?</summary><p>The workflow is agreed before launch. It can include calendar coordination, expectation setting, confirmation activity, CRM updates, and a structured handoff containing the account, contact, reason to meet, qualification notes, objections, timing, and agreed purpose.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam work inside our CRM?</summary><p>CallTeam can work with an agreed CRM or campaign workflow when access, permissions, fields, and operating rules are clear. If direct access is not appropriate, we can use another structured reporting and handoff process.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is CallTeam a pay-per-appointment provider?</summary><p>CallTeam scopes the work around the market, research, list quality, calling, follow-up, qualification, reporting, and management required. The commercial model is discussed after reviewing the campaign rather than treating every calendar booking as equal.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does CallTeam guarantee a number of appointments?</summary><p>No honest provider controls every list, buyer, offer, market, timing issue, or competitive condition. CallTeam defines the process, executes the work, reports the evidence, and improves the campaign. Any specific commercial commitments belong in the engagement agreement.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam set appointments in the United States?</summary><p>Yes. CallTeam supports B2B campaigns targeting the United States, North America, and other English-speaking markets, including companies entering the US market from abroad.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How much do B2B appointment setting services cost?</summary><p>Cost depends on the market, research required, condition of the prospect data, buyer seniority, calling and follow up workload, qualification depth, CRM workflow, confirmation process, reporting, and campaign duration. We review those factors before recommending a scope.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How long does a B2B appointment setting campaign take?</summary><p>Timing depends on the market, list quality, offer, buyer seniority, sales cycle, and amount of follow up required. Early responses create useful evidence, but a campaign needs enough time to work the market, learn from conversations, and improve the approach.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Which outreach channels support appointment setting?</summary><p>Human calling is central to the CallTeam approach. Approved email and appropriate follow up can support the conversation when the data, permissions, market, and campaign scope make those channels useful.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What happens if a prospect cancels or misses the meeting?</summary><p>We record the outcome, preserve the conversation context, follow up appropriately, and attempt to reschedule when the business reason remains credible. A missed meeting is not treated as a healthy handoff without proper follow up.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can appointment setting work with inbound or dormant CRM leads?</summary><p>Yes. Inbound leads, old CRM contacts, dormant opportunities, event contacts, and previous prospects can all be worked. When the main problem is an existing lead pool that nobody is following up, <a href="/services/lead-reactivation/">Lead Reactivation Services</a> is the more precise starting point.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is the difference between appointment setting and outsourced SDR services?</summary><p>Appointment setting centers on qualification, calendar booking, confirmation, and handoff. <a href="/services/outsourced-sdr/">Outsourced SDR Services</a> manage a wider ongoing front of sales function that can include account research, outreach, follow up, CRM activity, reporting, and market learning.</p></details>
        <details><summary>When is CallTeam not the right fit?</summary><p>The service may not be the right fit when the offer has no evidence of demand, the target buyer cannot be defined, nobody can take the meetings, the sales cycle cannot support human outreach, or the only objective is the cheapest possible calendar volume.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do we start?</summary><p>Start with a strategy call. We review the offer, target market, list or data sources, buyer roles, qualification standard, sales process, CRM workflow, and appointment goals before recommending the right campaign.</p></details>
      </div>
